WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.446
Stop saying just

00:00:01.258 --> 00:00:01.942
testing

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.527
Not all testing

00:00:03.339 --> 00:00:04.117
is the same

00:00:04.283 --> 00:00:05.273
tinkering checks what

00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:06.193
a program does

00:00:06.276 --> 00:00:07.466
and example tests

00:00:07.278 --> 00:00:08.527
pin down concrete

00:00:08.339 --> 00:00:09.742
cases like add

00:00:09.554 --> 00:00:10.851
three and five

00:00:10.674 --> 00:00:11.617
equals eight

00:00:12.142 --> 00:00:13.439
Property-based testing

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:14.146
goes further

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.609
throwing wild inputs

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.434
at code and

00:00:16.246 --> 00:00:16.976
it has caught

00:00:16.788 --> 00:00:17.684
real bugs in

00:00:17.496 --> 00:00:18.226
browsers

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:19.264
GCC

00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:21.281
SQLite and S3

00:00:21.806 --> 00:00:22.867
The post argues

00:00:22.679 --> 00:00:23.398
that a clearer

00:00:23.209 --> 00:00:24.742
vocabulary for testing

00:00:24.554 --> 00:00:25.945
stops teams talking

00:00:25.756 --> 00:00:26.688
past each other

00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:27.313
about what their

00:00:27.124 --> 00:00:28.928
tests actually prove

00:00:29.454 --> 00:00:30.468
Full story on

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:31.175
the dashboard
